                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f48a86fa-1d1c-3268-ac77-8c3730f6ebdf",
                        "entry_id": "9cf43e5b-2f2a-3dd4-9e52-214a01f9ef99",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Relatif à l’œuvre mathématique de Bernard Riemann."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ffde9ad3-e571-43f1-a401-b0ac3a57b058",
                                "sense_id": "f48a86fa-1d1c-3268-ac77-8c3730f6ebdf",
                                "text": "Il existe des variétés pseudo-riemanniennes, généralisant les variétés riemanniennes, qui en restent assez proche par bien des aspects, et qui permettent notamment de modéliser l’espace-temps en physique."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "1c2573f8-38c7-343f-85c0-1615ba6ec48e",
                        "entry_id": "9cf43e5b-2f2a-3dd4-9e52-214a01f9ef99",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Se dit de la géométrie des espaces courbes."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "especially"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "bf98910a-a805-48c8-8de2-24e33f665830",
                        "source_entry_id": "9cf43e5b-2f2a-3dd4-9e52-214a01f9ef99",
                        "target_lang_word": "Riemannian",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "Riemannian",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "484f6fd8-53d9-3984-a4d0-df5689e7e84d",
                                        "entry_id": "974af779-562a-3fc7-856f-59fc04759170",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of or relating to the work, or theory developed from the work, of German mathematician Bernhard Riemann, especially to Riemannian manifolds and Riemannian geometry."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "not-comparable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d637aa0a-602a-4d74-8dff-b536883b5b33",
                                                "sense_id": "484f6fd8-53d9-3984-a4d0-df5689e7e84d",
                                                "text": "where R is the Riemannian curvature of the metric g, regarded as an operatorname End(TM)-valued two form, and √ is the Pfaffian, which is an invariant polynomial defined on the Lie algebra of skew symmetric matrices in even dimensions."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "19901f04-dc93-4d0f-b3b9-9b1fb9a64406",
                                                "sense_id": "484f6fd8-53d9-3984-a4d0-df5689e7e84d",
                                                "text": "As the preferred metrics applied to symplectic forms are Kähler metrics one could ask for the Riemannian structure which would make the cone with the metric #92;overlineg#61;dt²#43;t²ᵍ together with the symplectic form #92;omega into a Kähler manifold. Then #92;overlineg and #92;omega define a complex structure #92;overline#92;Phi. Alternatively, one could ask for a Riemannian metric g on M which would define a Kähler metric h on #92;mathcalZ via a Riemannian submersion."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c1b676af-77c4-42e6-8505-8bdd718a5aba",
                                                "sense_id": "484f6fd8-53d9-3984-a4d0-df5689e7e84d",
                                                "text": "2012, Yves Carriere, Appendix A: Variations on Riemannian Flows, Pierre Molino, Riemannian Foliations, Springer, page 217,\nThe object of this appendix is to give a summary of known results on 1-dimensional oriented Riemannian Foliations."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "13c5e22d-23cc-30e9-9849-e3566a97cc8b",
                                        "entry_id": "974af779-562a-3fc7-856f-59fc04759170",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Relating to the musical theories of German theorist Hugo Riemann, particularly his theory of harmony, which is characterised by a system of \\\"harmonic dualism\\\"."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "not-comparable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "35048bcc-03ac-4705-a07a-b755a60ef621",
                                                "sense_id": "13c5e22d-23cc-30e9-9849-e3566a97cc8b",
                                                "text": "And not only theory: most central European composers of this century were schooled in Riemannian doctrine of one type or another."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9c3d3032-c5b0-473a-9f09-3555b09bbc9a",
                                                "sense_id": "13c5e22d-23cc-30e9-9849-e3566a97cc8b",
                                                "text": "Or take, for example, the rehabilitation by late-twentieth-century North American theorists of Riemannian Tonnetze as a means to navigate the voice-leading intricacies of much chromatic and post-chromatic music."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f48db8e4-0762-4dab-876e-ac7c1d5dace0",
                                                "sense_id": "13c5e22d-23cc-30e9-9849-e3566a97cc8b",
                                                "text": "The paper connects two notions originating from different branches of the recent mathematical music theory: the neo-Riemannian Tonnetz and the property of well-formedness from the theory of the generated scales."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
